  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 444 AM MST Tue Nov 10 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis morning, a shortwave trough is moving through Montana, \npushing out the previous zonal flow aloft to the east. This will \ncause a tightening pressure gradient, both at the surface and in the\nupper levels of the atmosphere, causing gusty winds across \nCentral and North-Central MT. 700 mb winds look to average across \nthe models around 60 kts, and while diurnal heating may allow some\nstronger winds aloft to mix down to the surface, cross-sectional \nanalyses does not support the peak 700 mb winds to consistently \nmix down. As such, while winds will be gusty throughout the day, \nwe are not expecting them to reach high wind criteria. Winds will \ndecrease this evening as the shortwave trough moves east and out \nof the CWA.\n\nOn Wednesday, a strong ridge of high pressure begins to build to \nthe west, gradually moving eastward through Montana until its axis\nis centered over the Montana/Dakota border by Friday afternoon.\nThis will allow for warm temperatures, dry conditions, and\noccasional breeziness across the CWA throughout the rest of the\nwork week. In fact, it will get so warm that there will be the \npotential for record highs. Wednesday through Friday, the \nmajority of Central and North/Central MT will see highs in the mid\nto upper 60s, with the valley locations of Southwest MT seeing \nhighs in the mid 50s to low 60s. \n\nBy Saturday, a large upper level trough will push the ridge\neastward as it makes its way east across the Pacific Coast, \nputting us in southerly flow aloft. This will allow temperatures\nto begin a gradual cooling trend. Increased chances of\nprecipitation will spread across the southern half of the CWA this\nweekend as a surge of upper level moisture gets carried along by\nthe aforementioned trough. There is still a decent amount of\nuncertainty with how this trough will progress this weekend, as\nonly about 60% of models show the CWA under strong influence of \nthe trough on Sunday. -Dzomba\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe strongest winds today will be along the Rocky Mountain Front,\nparticularly along the immediate eastern slopes, where gusts \naround 60 MPH will be common. Across the more wind prone areas of \nthe Front, gusts in excess of 70 MPH cannot be ruled out. The \nprobability of a gust greater than 75 MPH around Browning still \nonly peaks at around 20%.\n\nFergus, Judith Basin, Meagher and Cascade Counties will see the \nsecond strongest winds, with gusts 45-50 MPH. A few isolated gusts\nof high wind criteria (58+ MPH) are possible, but are still too \nisolated to warrant any high wind warnings. The rest of \nCentral/North-Central MT will see gusts around 35 MPH, while \nsouthwest MT will only see general breeziness with gusts around 25\nMPH. -Dzomba",
